The Greenland Sea ice is ~50 % too fast, and the EVP test (128) shows its velocity converged, so the ice moves with what
drives it. This compares, model against GLORYS, each on its own grid (speeds and the slip do not depend on the grid's
axes): the ice speed, the top-level ocean speed under the ice, the slip |uᵢ − uₒ| and the top-level ocean speed over
the whole region; area-weighted over ice cells (concentration > 15 %) of 65–82°N, 40°W–15°E, every n days of a run's
daily averages. GLORYS's uo, vo (top level), siconc, usi, vsi are read directly from its daily files over a box around
the region. greenland_ice_drift.md (means over each half, then each day) and greenland_ice_drift.png (three time series
and the distribution of the slip). CPU; downloads the GLORYS days it needs.

